Hydrogen is central to decarbonising hard-to-electrify sectors and enabling long-term, large-scale energy storage. Therefore, the use of hydrogen – already prominent in processes such as refining, ammonia production and steelmaking – is expected to significantly broaden over the next decade [ 1 ]. As a result, a vast number of structural components, containment systems (e.g. pressure vessels, storage tanks), and pipelines will be in contact with hydrogen, typically in its gaseous form (H 2 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
